WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.000
- [Narrator] King Charles III
is the UK's new head of state

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.130
and head of the royal family.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.350
He's basically the CEO
of the British monarchy,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.060
and he has said that
one of his priorities is

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.743
to bring down running costs.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.140
Last year, the monarchy
cost the government

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.790
roughly 102 million pounds,
the equivalent of $117 million

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.120
according to the palace's
annual financial report.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:21.953
So, here's what we know

00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:24.483
about where that money comes
from and how it's spent.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.610
- I take this opportunity
to continue the tradition

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.430
of surrendering the hereditary revenues,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.730
including the crown
estate to my government

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.560
for the benefit of all.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.540
- [Narrator] The cost of carrying
out official duties comes

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.580
from something called the sovereign grant.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:42.843
Here's how that works.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.690
In the 1700s, the monarchy handed

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.160
over revenues from swathes of land

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.590
known as the crown
estate to the government.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.240
These days, the crown estate makes money

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.190
on a range of assets from retail property

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.280
to golf courses and even
offshore wind farms.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.930
And every year the
government pays a percentage

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.720
of the profits made on those
assets back to the monarchy.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.290
Last year, it totaled $99.6 million.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.880
In addition to their vast private wealth

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.220
and income from investments,
which are not included here,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.500
the monarchy also received $11.4 million

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.170
from royal palace admissions
and rental properties.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.660
This slice of income is used
to supplement the grant.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.400
But last year, the monarchy
also tapped a reserve

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.610
of grant money from past years
to meet its spending needs.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.080
So, where did that money go?

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.250
The bulk of this spending goes

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.380
on the monarch's operating
costs, official travel,

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.520
and property maintenance.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.010
In total, more than $3
million was spent last year

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.680
on helicopter trips and
journeys on the royal train.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.060
And 10 times more than that was used

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.910
to employ about 1,000 people,
from footmen to florists,

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.470
who make up the royal household,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.570
headquartered at Buckingham Palace.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.730
The palace itself has high running costs,

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.920
and money spent on renovating it rose 41%

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.070
from the previous year.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.510
A 10-year project to refurbish
Buckingham Palace is forecast

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.270
to cost around $426 million

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.670
according to the last financial report.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.440
And there are costs we don't know.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.570
Some royal watchers say the
bill is higher than stated

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.360
because of the costs of police
protection for the family,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.770
and the cost of the
queen's funeral couldn't

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.070
immediately be determined.

00:02:04.843 --> 00:02:05.940
- [Protesters] Freeze profits, not people.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:06.900
- [Narrator] How this money is spent is

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.400
particularly pertinent at a time

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.690
when people are voicing concerns

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.190
about soaring energy bills,

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:14.850
and inflation in the UK is
at its highest in 40 years.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.490
- Her Majesty's government's priority is

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.160
to grow and strengthen the economy

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.167
and help ease the cost
of living for families.

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:25.650
- [Narrator] $117 million
of taxpayer money works out

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:30.330
at about $1.73 or £1.50 per UK citizen.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.480
King Charles seemingly wants
it to be even less than that.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.710
Demonstrating that the monarchy is

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.120
good value for money is more difficult

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.460
in tougher economic times,
and Charles' plans are

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:39.810
part of a careful balancing act

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.020
deftly performed by his
mother for 70 years.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.151
Now, it's his turn to
walk that tight rope.

00:02:45.151 --> 00:02:48.401
(pensive string music)

